1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,194
Your agent wrote four
thousand lines this week.

2
00:00:03,194 --> 00:00:05,640
Code, tests, docs, a migration plan.

3
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:07,547
How much of it did you read?

4
00:00:07,547 --> 00:00:11,340
There is a whole category of software
that AI is not allowed to write.

5
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:15,520
Aircraft. Medical devices. Not
because the models are bad at it.

6
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:18,900
Because nobody can check the work fast
enough to keep the standards intact.

7
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:23,800
AI made writing code cheap. It
did not make trusting code cheap.

8
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:28,040
And that is a verification problem,
which means I think it is solvable.

9
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:32,092
This is not a thought experiment,
either. Across the industry,

10
00:00:32,092 --> 00:00:34,820
more and more of the work
already happens agentically.

11
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:38,280
Pull requests arrive faster than
any team can actually read them.

12
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:40,681
The pace becomes the
product, and review is

13
00:00:40,681 --> 00:00:42,460
the thing that gets crushed underneath it.

14
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:45,080
Because serious software is
not just software that runs.

15
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:49,541
Serious software is software where
someone can explain what it is supposed to

16
00:00:49,541 --> 00:00:53,789
do, what requirement it satisfies, what
test verified it, what version the test

17
00:00:53,789 --> 00:00:57,500
ran against, what evidence was
produced, and who accepted the result.

18
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:00,668
Let me be concrete about
what I mean, because

19
00:01:00,668 --> 00:01:02,720
there are really two
different worlds here.

20
00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:05,400
In one of them, quality
is a business problem.

21
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:09,220
A web application has to be reliable
enough that customers do not leave.

22
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:14,200
If something breaks on Monday, you ship
a fix on Tuesday, and life goes on.

23
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:16,840
In the other one, software flies aircraft.

24
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,300
It keeps satellites talking to the ground.

25
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:22,960
It decides how hard your car brakes
when a child steps into the road.

26
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:27,620
That software is certified before it is
allowed to run, and somebody has to be

27
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:31,420
accountable for it — an organization,
a process, a regulator signing off.

28
00:01:32,540 --> 00:01:34,260
AI has made generation faster.

29
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:38,860
It has not automatically made verification
faster. So the bottleneck moves.

30
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:42,320
Not to the editor. Not
to the model. To trust.

31
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:44,841
And that is why I have been thinking about

32
00:01:44,841 --> 00:01:46,560
something I call a Verification Compiler.

33
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:51,100
The goal is not to bring AI into
safety-critical work for the sake of it.

34
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:54,958
The goal is to automate the
verification scaffolding during

35
00:01:54,958 --> 00:01:58,101
development, so that the
standards that keep that software

36
00:01:58,101 --> 00:02:00,540
safe stay fully intact
while you move faster.

37
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:06,480
It is a process, not a shortcut. Or,
less formally: a compiler for trust.

38
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:10,473
Here is the trap. You ask
a model to write some code.

39
00:02:10,953 --> 00:02:14,133
Then you ask the same model whether
the code satisfies the requirement.

40
00:02:15,153 --> 00:02:19,453
It says yes, with a calm and often
genuinely useful explanation.

41
00:02:20,093 --> 00:02:25,813
But what do you actually have? No
proof. No certification. No audit trail.

42
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:30,913
You have a chat transcript. And a
chat transcript is not evidence.

43
00:02:31,253 --> 00:02:33,013
For a quick iteration, that may be fine.

44
00:02:33,593 --> 00:02:35,954
Where failure actually
matters, the question is

45
00:02:35,954 --> 00:02:38,013
not whether the answer sounds reasonable.

46
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:42,193
The question is: which requirement
is being checked? Which code version?

47
00:02:42,493 --> 00:02:45,833
Which test result? Which source
document? Which review rule?

48
00:02:46,953 --> 00:02:51,273
Which human accepted it? And can we
reproduce what was reviewed later?

49
00:02:51,873 --> 00:02:54,473
That is the gap. The model
can generate an answer.

50
00:02:54,813 --> 00:02:57,073
But the answer still has
to be turned into evidence.

51
00:02:58,407 --> 00:03:01,347
High-assurance software has been dealing
with this kind of problem for decades.

52
00:03:01,747 --> 00:03:04,807
And I think most developers
have no idea this world exists.

53
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:09,047
There are entire standards governing how
this software is allowed to be built.

54
00:03:10,167 --> 00:03:15,607
DO-178C in aerospace. ISO 26262,
and ASIL levels, in automotive.

55
00:03:16,147 --> 00:03:18,067
There are more, industry by industry.

56
00:03:18,547 --> 00:03:20,683
The details vary, and
the standards themselves

57
00:03:20,683 --> 00:03:22,727
are careful legal and
engineering documents.

58
00:03:23,107 --> 00:03:25,187
But the broad pattern
is simple enough to see.

59
00:03:26,147 --> 00:03:30,407
You do not just write code and
hope. You maintain traceability.

60
00:03:30,987 --> 00:03:36,507
A requirement traces to design. Design
traces to code. Code traces to tests.

61
00:03:37,107 --> 00:03:41,267
Tests trace to results. Results
trace to review records.

62
00:03:41,807 --> 00:03:45,987
And all of that has to stay tied to the
version of the system being evaluated.

63
00:03:46,827 --> 00:03:49,647
That can sound like
bureaucracy from the outside.

64
00:03:50,207 --> 00:03:53,207
And I want to be honest about
it: it is a lot of overhead work.

65
00:03:53,207 --> 00:03:56,602
It is part of why the same
function can cost a hundred

66
00:03:56,602 --> 00:03:59,747
times more to ship on an
aircraft than on a drone.

67
00:04:00,167 --> 00:04:03,627
But the deeper idea is not
bureaucracy. It is coordination.

68
00:04:05,107 --> 00:04:08,056
It is a way for a large
group of limited humans

69
00:04:08,056 --> 00:04:10,567
to make a complicated system reviewable.

70
00:04:10,947 --> 00:04:12,927
And reviewable is the word that matters.

71
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:17,147
Nobody can look at a whole system
at once and pronounce it good.

72
00:04:17,727 --> 00:04:21,247
It is too big. That is true
of every engineer on the team.

73
00:04:21,247 --> 00:04:25,187
It is just as true of an AI
model asked to do the same thing.

74
00:04:25,527 --> 00:04:28,847
So you break it apart. You
verify the small units first.

75
00:04:29,967 --> 00:04:34,487
Unit tests. Integration tests.
System tests. Verification tests.

76
00:04:34,967 --> 00:04:37,812
And the requirements stack
the same way, sub-requirements

77
00:04:37,812 --> 00:04:40,507
rolling up into the requirements
that actually matter.

78
00:04:41,007 --> 00:04:44,119
You climb that ladder until
the system is as true as you

79
00:04:44,119 --> 00:04:46,787
can make it, before
anyone's life depends on it.

80
00:04:47,747 --> 00:04:50,327
And that is the clue for
AI-assisted software.

81
00:04:50,327 --> 00:04:55,009
If models are going to generate more
artifacts, faster, then the project

82
00:04:55,009 --> 00:04:59,627
needs an even stronger structure for
knowing what those artifacts mean.

83
00:05:00,067 --> 00:05:04,367
The first move is to stop thinking of
a project as a folder full of files.

84
00:05:04,807 --> 00:05:09,267
For verification, a project is a graph.
Every important thing becomes a node.

85
00:05:09,827 --> 00:05:14,607
Requirements. Source code units. Tests.
Test results. Static analysis results.

86
00:05:14,827 --> 00:05:18,287
Datasheets. Interface control
documents. Physical test records.

87
00:05:18,807 --> 00:05:21,007
Model review outputs.
Human review outputs.

88
00:05:22,187 --> 00:05:26,197
Even specific sections of source
documents become nodes, because a

89
00:05:26,197 --> 00:05:30,147
citation that only says "see the
standard" is not precise enough.

90
00:05:30,947 --> 00:05:35,507
Then the relationships become edges.
This requirement traces to that code.

91
00:05:35,847 --> 00:05:40,827
This test verifies that behavior. This
result was produced against this version.

92
00:05:41,787 --> 00:05:46,347
This source region supports this
claim. Now the project has a shape.

93
00:05:46,767 --> 00:05:49,627
And once it has a shape, you
can ask structural questions.

94
00:05:50,627 --> 00:05:53,007
Is there code with no requirement?

95
00:05:53,367 --> 00:05:55,587
Is there a requirement
with no implementation?

96
00:05:56,247 --> 00:05:58,547
Is there a test result
tied to an old version?

97
00:05:59,527 --> 00:06:02,807
Is there a model review that
disagrees with a human review?

98
00:06:03,467 --> 00:06:07,287
Those are not philosophical questions
anymore. They are graph questions.

99
00:06:08,117 --> 00:06:11,257
This is where the compiler
analogy starts to matter.

100
00:06:11,797 --> 00:06:15,074
A normal compiler takes source
code, walks a structured

101
00:06:15,074 --> 00:06:17,517
representation, and emits something else.

102
00:06:18,317 --> 00:06:21,576
It turns one representation
into another representation

103
00:06:21,576 --> 00:06:23,117
through a defined process.

104
00:06:24,557 --> 00:06:28,137
A Verification Compiler would
not compile code into a binary.

105
00:06:28,757 --> 00:06:31,997
It would compile an artifact
graph into verification work.

106
00:06:32,497 --> 00:06:35,297
Start with a top-level
requirement. Traverse the graph.

107
00:06:35,697 --> 00:06:38,977
Collect the code units that matter.
Collect the linked requirements.

108
00:06:39,337 --> 00:06:42,797
Collect the tests. Collect the results.
Collect the relevant source text.

109
00:06:43,657 --> 00:06:45,777
Collect the static analysis output.

110
00:06:46,437 --> 00:06:50,317
Then assemble that into a bounded
review package. Same graph state.

111
00:06:50,677 --> 00:06:55,377
Same traversal rule. Same package. That
deterministic assembly is the point.

112
00:06:55,977 --> 00:06:59,277
The model might be probabilistic.
The human might be inconsistent.

113
00:06:59,837 --> 00:07:02,437
But the package they receive
should not be a vibe.

114
00:07:02,877 --> 00:07:04,557
It should be a reproducible artifact.

115
00:07:05,117 --> 00:07:09,797
I have been calling that artifact a
Verification Query Package. A VQP.

116
00:07:10,477 --> 00:07:12,577
The name is clunky,
but the idea is useful.

117
00:07:13,177 --> 00:07:15,902
A Verification Query
Package is the smallest unit

118
00:07:15,902 --> 00:07:18,517
of verification work that
can stand on its own.

119
00:07:19,097 --> 00:07:20,597
It includes the code under review.

120
00:07:20,997 --> 00:07:23,677
It includes the requirements
that code is supposed to satisfy.

121
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:27,057
It includes the tests and results
attached to the same version.

122
00:07:27,517 --> 00:07:30,877
It includes the source-region text
that supports the claim being checked.

123
00:07:30,877 --> 00:07:34,417
It includes the review
instructions. And it is bounded.

124
00:07:35,397 --> 00:07:39,477
Small enough for a model to analyze.
Small enough for a human to inspect.

125
00:07:39,937 --> 00:07:43,577
But complete enough that the reviewer
is not guessing what context matters.

126
00:07:44,437 --> 00:07:46,617
And a package does not have to be text.

127
00:07:47,317 --> 00:07:51,362
A rendered screen, a captured output,
a physical test trace — if it is

128
00:07:51,362 --> 00:07:55,177
evidence about the thing being
checked, it belongs in the package.

129
00:07:56,237 --> 00:07:59,713
That matters well beyond aerospace,
though aerospace is where the

130
00:07:59,713 --> 00:08:03,137
standards are strictest, and that
is the case this is built for.

131
00:08:03,997 --> 00:08:06,137
This is one of the important shifts.

132
00:08:06,757 --> 00:08:11,053
Instead of asking a model to review an
entire project, or asking a human to search

133
00:08:11,053 --> 00:08:14,617
through a pile of artifacts, the
graph assembles the relevant slice.

134
00:08:15,497 --> 00:08:18,357
The huge problem becomes
many smaller review packages.

135
00:08:18,857 --> 00:08:21,497
And each package can leave
behind a structured result.

136
00:08:21,867 --> 00:08:25,147
This also gives us a cleaner
way to talk about AI review.

137
00:08:25,767 --> 00:08:29,187
The same package can go to a model.
The same package can go to a human.

138
00:08:29,667 --> 00:08:33,147
The input structure is the same.
The output schema is the same.

139
00:08:33,287 --> 00:08:34,987
But the authority is not the same.

140
00:08:35,447 --> 00:08:37,787
In development, a model can
review packages quickly.

141
00:08:38,087 --> 00:08:40,827
It can flag missing evidence.
It can notice mismatches.

142
00:08:41,107 --> 00:08:44,707
It can say: this requirement looks only
partially covered. That is valuable.

143
00:08:45,147 --> 00:08:46,667
But it is developmental review.

144
00:08:47,527 --> 00:08:51,318
For certification-facing decisions,
a human reviewer can inspect the

145
00:08:51,318 --> 00:08:55,276
same package — rendered into something
actually readable, a page rather

146
00:08:55,276 --> 00:08:58,287
than a blob of JSON — and
produce the accepted result.

147
00:08:59,147 --> 00:09:02,627
If the model and the human disagree,
the disagreement is not swept away.

148
00:09:03,067 --> 00:09:04,687
It becomes part of the evidence history.

149
00:09:05,187 --> 00:09:07,807
It can update the model's
suitability for that kind of task.

150
00:09:08,207 --> 00:09:11,407
The system is not trying to pretend
the model is a perfect certifier.

151
00:09:11,727 --> 00:09:14,855
It is trying to make model
assistance fit inside

152
00:09:14,855 --> 00:09:17,527
a review structure that humans can audit.

153
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:21,510
The output should not be a chat bubble.
It should be an evidence record.

154
00:09:21,910 --> 00:09:23,310
And a record has fields.

155
00:09:24,530 --> 00:09:28,670
Every one of those fields answers
a question you would otherwise have

156
00:09:28,670 --> 00:09:32,690
to walk over and ask somebody: what
was decided, why, what supports

157
00:09:32,690 --> 00:09:35,990
it, who decided, and against
which version of the code.

158
00:09:36,530 --> 00:09:39,603
And once that record exists, it
should not be edited in place.

159
00:09:39,930 --> 00:09:43,810
If the code changes, the old evidence
does not disappear. It becomes stale.

160
00:09:44,310 --> 00:09:46,858
If the source document
changes, links depending

161
00:09:46,858 --> 00:09:49,190
on that source region
need to be rechecked.

162
00:09:50,170 --> 00:09:53,710
If a human overrides a model
result, both records stay visible.

163
00:09:54,310 --> 00:09:57,630
This is how you get an audit trail
instead of a pile of confident text.

164
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:00,610
The point is not that every
record is true forever.

165
00:10:00,990 --> 00:10:03,270
The point is that every
record has provenance.

166
00:10:03,767 --> 00:10:07,427
Once the project is a graph and the
reviews are structured, you can zoom out.

167
00:10:07,787 --> 00:10:10,107
You can ask: how much of
the graph has been reviewed?

168
00:10:10,407 --> 00:10:13,507
Which requirements have current
evidence? Which evidence is stale?

169
00:10:13,747 --> 00:10:15,147
Which parts are missing tests?

170
00:10:15,367 --> 00:10:18,767
Which model results are below the
suitability threshold for this task?

171
00:10:19,207 --> 00:10:20,907
Which areas still need human attention?

172
00:10:21,467 --> 00:10:24,287
That gives you something like a
Verification Readiness Metric.

173
00:10:25,367 --> 00:10:28,347
But this is important: it
is not a magic safety score.

174
00:10:28,767 --> 00:10:31,487
It does not say the system is
safe because the number is high.

175
00:10:31,487 --> 00:10:34,528
It says the evidence surface
is more or less complete,

176
00:10:34,528 --> 00:10:37,007
more or less current,
more or less reviewed.

177
00:10:37,787 --> 00:10:39,467
It makes the gaps visible.

178
00:10:40,767 --> 00:10:44,847
That is what readiness means
here. Not certainty. Visibility.

179
00:10:45,447 --> 00:10:48,333
And one more distinction,
because the two words get used

180
00:10:48,333 --> 00:10:50,807
interchangeably and they
are not the same thing.

181
00:10:51,947 --> 00:10:55,447
Verification is checking that you
built the thing you meant to build.

182
00:10:56,247 --> 00:10:59,754
Validation is checking that the
thing you built is the thing you

183
00:10:59,754 --> 00:11:03,207
should have built — that it
actually holds up out in the world.

184
00:11:04,147 --> 00:11:05,787
Those are two separate processes.

185
00:11:06,427 --> 00:11:09,487
The Verification Compiler is
aimed squarely at the first one.

186
00:11:09,987 --> 00:11:12,107
It will not tell you that you
designed the right system.

187
00:11:12,507 --> 00:11:15,487
It tells you whether you can show that
you built the system you specified.

188
00:11:16,067 --> 00:11:18,127
I think this is where
AI software is going.

189
00:11:18,627 --> 00:11:22,107
Not because models will magically
replace verification. They will not.

190
00:11:22,487 --> 00:11:25,601
But because generation is
getting cheaper, and every cheap

191
00:11:25,601 --> 00:11:28,287
generation step creates a
verification obligation.

192
00:11:28,887 --> 00:11:32,067
Another function. Another test.
Another design note. Another claim.

193
00:11:32,367 --> 00:11:33,767
Another assumption.

194
00:11:34,327 --> 00:11:38,307
The bottleneck becomes: what can we trust
enough to connect back into the world?

195
00:11:38,827 --> 00:11:41,827
The Verification Compiler is
one answer to that bottleneck.

196
00:11:42,267 --> 00:11:45,507
Turn the project into a graph.
Traverse the graph deterministically.

197
00:11:46,607 --> 00:11:50,867
Assemble bounded review packages.
Let models help during development.

198
00:11:51,247 --> 00:11:53,127
Let humans retain
authority where it matters.

199
00:11:53,607 --> 00:11:56,687
And preserve the result as
evidence. Not "trust the AI."

200
00:11:57,287 --> 00:11:58,687
Trust the evidence surface.

201
00:11:59,007 --> 00:12:01,787
Because the future of AI software
is not just generating code.

202
00:12:02,327 --> 00:12:04,567
It is generating evidence
we can actually inspect.

203
00:12:05,017 --> 00:12:06,697
Thank you for sitting
through my thinking on this.

204
00:12:07,097 --> 00:12:09,975
The full whitepaper that goes with
this video is in the description,

205
00:12:09,975 --> 00:12:12,177
along with the references
behind the follow-on work.

206
00:12:12,857 --> 00:12:15,901
And if you are an agentic developer,
use those resources to have

207
00:12:15,901 --> 00:12:18,755
your agent build its own version
of a Verification Compiler.

208
00:12:18,755 --> 00:12:21,277
Then tell me in the comments
how the experiment went.

209
00:12:22,377 --> 00:12:25,851
What tagging system did you use?
How did you specify requirements

210
00:12:25,851 --> 00:12:29,271
and sub-requirements? What did
you use for unique, hyperlinkable

211
00:12:29,271 --> 00:12:31,677
IDs — if you even found
that you needed them?

212
00:12:32,477 --> 00:12:36,232
I wanted to publish this idea so that
other people could start expanding on

213
00:12:36,232 --> 00:12:39,737
what is possible with it. I am
genuinely excited to see where it goes.

214
00:12:40,417 --> 00:12:44,212
I want to be straight about what this
is, though. It is a whitepaper-level

215
00:12:44,212 --> 00:12:46,777
idea. It is not a product,
and it is not finished.

216
00:12:47,617 --> 00:12:52,327
So the next thing I am going to do is
build it. I want to practise implementing

217
00:12:52,327 --> 00:12:56,918
this, refine it, and find the rough
edges — because the rough edges are where

218
00:12:56,918 --> 00:13:00,317
an idea like this either holds
up or quietly falls apart.

219
00:13:00,317 --> 00:13:04,137
If you want to know when that
lands, like the video and subscribe.

220
00:13:04,897 --> 00:13:08,660
And if you know someone working
on this problem — someone who

221
00:13:08,660 --> 00:13:12,423
has to get software through a
certification process — send it

222
00:13:12,423 --> 00:13:15,817
to them. That is the conversation
I am hoping to start.
